body:
READ-ONLY FOLLOW-UP: the locator/denominator classification needs a third label at the align boundary: ABSENCE-ERASING DERIVATION. I inspected current source, made no edit, and claim no bead. SourceView.relativeSpan is indeed the single denominator calculation at align/source.scala:245-247. But relativePosition immediately maps a missing span to 0.0 at :251-252. TransitionFeatures.between consumes that bare Double for Backward and LongJump; RecallSignature and PopulationAggregate wrap it as Some(view.relativePosition(...)); embed-bench does likewise after checking only that the node exists. Once erased, missing support and the beginning of the presentation become observationally identical. Two newly filed P2 beads currently name this exact defect (bd-01M1789HG7YH0R8A8V8CH4GTEJ and bd-01M1789P5B9WVNCJFN3HXACWKN); they appear duplicate and I have not claimed either.

MINIMAL ADDITIVE SHAPE FOR STAGE A: keep heterogeneous EvidenceSupport separate from one explicit PrimaryPresentationAxis owned by SourceView. The axis should carry both SourceKind and a source/stream identity, and should expose comparison/distance operations rather than handing consumers an untagged normalized Double. A path-dependent axis member is the smallest type-level shape I see that avoids parameterizing NarrativeGraph or StoryModel: a view owns axis: PresentationAxis with an abstract Kind; positions produced by that axis are RelativePosition[axis.Kind], so two positions from one view compare, while text-character and film-PTS coordinates cannot be passed to one comparison. Evidence for one claim may still cite text, frames, and audio simultaneously; aligner order is a distinct projection onto the declared primary axis.

ADR LAWS I RECOMMEND PINNING:
1. axis identity (kind, source/stream id, extent semantics) participates in the SourceView fingerprint and every result proof;
2. text-character and media-PTS positions have no common unchecked comparison, even though both render in [0,1];
3. missing position remains None/typed missing through transition, signature, population, and benchmark consumers - never 0.0 and never Some(default);
4. mixed evidence never chooses presentation order implicitly; the bridge records which primary-axis interval each narrative node projects onto and with what receipt;
5. text remains CharacterOffset/CanonicalLength and film remains PresentationTimestamp/Runtime, so a normalized value is a rendering of a typed coordinate, not its identity.

This supports the chief ruling without widening core now. It also gives the ADR a discriminating negative example: text 0.5 and film 0.5 must fail to type-check or return a typed axis mismatch, while two positions emitted by the same view remain comparable. The current bare-Double seam would pass the wrong program.
